/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Critical Thinking (batch z) */
WB.L("illusory-truth-effect", "thinking", "beginner", "The illusory truth effect: why repetition feels like proof",
  "Hear something often enough and it starts to feel true, even when you know better. Here's why your brain falls for it, and how to fight back.",
  [
    ["Repetition feels true", "Hearing a claim again makes it feel truer, because familiar ideas are easier for the brain to process.",
      "In 1977 psychologists Lynn Hasher, David Goldstein and Thomas Toppino had students rate trivia statements for truth over three sessions, two weeks apart. Some statements returned every session; others were new each time. The repeated ones were rated more likely to be true each time, whether they were true or not. Nothing about the evidence had changed. Only familiarity had. The leading explanation is processing fluency: a statement you have met before is easier to read and understand, and the brain treats that ease as a sign of accuracy. It is often a decent clue, but fluency can come from almost anywhere. Easier-to-read colours, and rhymes such as ‘woes unite foes’, also make statements seem truer."],
    ["Knowing better isn't enough", "Repetition can nudge belief even in claims that contradict what you already know.",
      "It is tempting to think this only works on topics you know nothing about. In a 2015 study led by Lisa Fazio, participants read statements such as ‘A sari is the name of the short pleated skirt worn by Scots’, which contradicts something most of them knew: that skirt is a kilt. Even so, reading a false statement more than once made people rate it as somewhat more true than they otherwise would. Knowledge helped, but it didn't switch the effect off. People often failed to bring what they knew to mind and relied on the feeling of familiarity instead. The biggest jump in belief tends to come from the second exposure, with smaller gains after that."],
    ["Fake news and repetition", "Even a single earlier glimpse of a false headline can make it seem more accurate later, warning label or not.",
      "In 2018 Gordon Pennycook, Tyrone Cannon and David Rand showed people real fake-news headlines styled as they would appear in a feed. Headlines people had seen before were rated as more accurate than new ones, after just one earlier exposure. Strikingly, the effect still appeared when the headlines had carried a warning label saying they were disputed by fact-checkers. This matters because online platforms are repetition machines. A claim can reach you through a friend's post, a meme, a video and a comment thread in the same afternoon, and each one makes it feel a little more familiar. Advertisers and propagandists have understood the power of repetition for a very long time."],
    ["The trouble with myth-busting", "Correct a myth by leading with the truth, so the fact rather than the myth becomes the familiar thing.",
      "Correcting a falsehood is a headache. A typical myth-busting article repeats the myth, sometimes in large type, then explains why it's wrong. Readers may later remember the familiar claim and forget the correction. Researchers once worried that corrections might routinely ‘backfire’ and strengthen false beliefs, but later work found this is rare; corrections generally help. Still, how you correct matters. A widely recommended approach, sometimes called a ‘truth sandwich’, starts with the accurate fact, mentions the myth once and clearly labels it false, explains why it's wrong, then finishes by repeating the truth. That way the fact gets the repetition and becomes the familiar one."],
    ["Defending your own mind", "Treat ‘I've heard that before’ as a cue to check the evidence, not as a conclusion.",
      "You can't stop repetition from affecting you, but you can make it work less well. Notice the feeling of ‘I've heard that before’ and treat it as a cue, not a conclusion. Ask where you heard it, whether the source was reliable, and whether you have ever seen actual evidence. Some studies suggest that prompting people to focus on accuracy, or to think about how they know a claim is true, reduces the effect, especially for claims they have the knowledge to check. It also helps to be careful about what you repeat yourself. Every time you pass on a dubious claim, even as a joke, you add one more exposure to someone else's sense that it's true."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does a repeated statement tend to feel more true?", ["Each repeat gives the brain fresh evidence for it","People tend to repeat only claims that are true","Familiar claims feel easy to process, so seem true","Repetition makes people check the facts more carefully"], 2, "Processing fluency makes familiar claims feel smooth, and the brain reads smoothness as truth."],
    [1, "What happened when people repeatedly read false statements that contradicted their own knowledge?", ["Repetition still made them seem somewhat more true","They rejected them more firmly each time","They grew sceptical of every statement they read","They permanently forgot the correct fact"], 0, "Knowing better helps, but familiarity can still nudge belief when we fail to bring our knowledge to mind."],
    [2, "Why are online platforms a good environment for the illusory truth effect?", ["They only show claims that have been checked","Warning labels completely cancel the effect","People rarely see the same claim more than once","A claim can reach you many times in one afternoon"], 3, "Feeds repeat claims constantly, and even a disputed label does not stop familiarity from working."],
    [3, "What is the ‘truth sandwich’ approach to correcting a myth?", ["Repeat the myth several times so people notice it","Open with the fact, name the myth once, end with the fact","Ignore the myth and hope people forget it","Point out how foolish believers of the myth are"], 1, "Putting the truth at the start and end makes the fact, not the myth, the thing that gets repeated."],
    [4, "What is a good response to the feeling of ‘I've heard that before’?", ["Ask where you heard it and look for evidence","Treat it as proof the claim is true","Ignore all claims you have heard before","Repeat the claim to test it on others"], 0, "Familiarity is a cue to check the source and evidence, not a conclusion."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Why a claim you've heard a few times feels truer, even when you know better."});

WB.L("wason-confirmation-bias", "thinking", "beginner", "Confirmation bias: the puzzle almost everyone gets wrong",
  "Three numbers, one hidden rule, and a mistake that reveals how we all hunt for proof we're right rather than for the truth.",
  [
    ["The 2-4-6 puzzle", "Most people test a rule only with examples that fit their theory, so they never see it fail.",
      "In 1960 the British psychologist Peter Wason, at University College London, gave volunteers three numbers: 2, 4, 6. The sequence followed a rule he had in mind, and their job was to discover it. They could propose their own sets of three numbers, and he would say whether each fitted. Most people quickly formed a theory, such as ‘even numbers going up by two’, and tested it with sets like 8, 10, 12. Every one earned a yes, so they announced their rule, and many were told they were wrong. The real rule was simply ‘any three numbers in increasing order’. Tests like 8, 10, 12 fit both the theory and the real rule, so they could never reveal the mistake. Those who succeeded tried sequences their theory said should fail, such as 1, 2, 3."],
    ["Four cards on a table", "In Wason's selection task, most people turn over the wrong cards, because they look for confirmation rather than a possible break.",
      "Wason's most famous puzzle followed in 1966. Four cards each have a letter on one side and a number on the other. You can see E, K, 4 and 7. The rule is: ‘If a card has a vowel on one side, it has an even number on the other.’ Which cards must you turn over to check whether the rule is being broken? Most people pick E, and often 4 as well. E is right. But 4 tells you nothing, because the rule says nothing about what must be behind an even number. The card you need is 7: if a vowel is hiding behind it, the rule is broken. In many studies, only around one person in ten chooses correctly."],
    ["Easy when it's about cheating", "The same logic becomes simple when the puzzle is about catching rule-breakers in a familiar setting.",
      "Here's the strange part. Researchers later rewrote the puzzle in familiar terms. Imagine you are checking a bar where the rule is: ‘If someone is drinking beer, they must be over 18.’ The four cards show: drinking beer, drinking cola, age 25 and age 16. Which do you check? Most people quickly pick the beer drinker and the 16-year-old, which is logically the same answer as E and 7. Studies by Richard Griggs and James Cox in 1982 found that versions like this are solved far more often than the abstract one. Psychologists still debate why. Some argue our minds are tuned to spot social cheating; others say familiar situations simply make it obvious which cases could break the rule."],
    ["Confirmation bias everywhere", "Confirmation bias is the habit of seeking, noticing and remembering information that supports what we already believe.",
      "Wason's puzzles became classic demonstrations of confirmation bias: the tendency to search for, interpret and remember information in ways that support what we already think. It shows up far beyond the laboratory. A detective who settles on a suspect early may give extra weight to clues that point their way. Someone convinced a colleague dislikes them reads every short email as proof. Investors can seek out news that flatters the shares they already own. Online, search engines and feeds make it easy to find something that agrees with almost any belief. The bias isn't stupidity. Holding on to a working theory saves effort, and often the theory is right. The danger is that we rarely notice when it isn't."],
    ["Try to break your idea", "The best test of a belief is one that could prove it wrong.",
      "The lesson from Wason is practical. When you have a theory, ask: what would I expect to see if I were wrong, and have I looked for it? Scientists build this into their method by designing experiments that could fail. You can do a small version in daily life. Before a big purchase, read the worst reviews, not just the best. Before deciding someone is untrustworthy, list the evidence that points the other way. In a 1984 study, Charles Lord and colleagues found that asking people to deliberately consider the opposite reduced biased judgements more effectively than simply telling them to be fair and unbiased. Testing your ideas like this feels uncomfortable, but it's the quickest route to the truth."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did testing a set like 8, 10, 12 fail to help people in the 2-4-6 task?", ["The numbers were too large to judge fairly","Wason gave misleading answers to some tests","It fitted both their theory and the true rule","It was rejected because it broke the real rule"], 2, "A test that your theory and the true rule both pass cannot tell them apart."],
    [1, "In the card task showing E, K, 4 and 7, which cards must be turned over to check the rule?", ["E and 4","E and 7","K and 4","4 and 7"], 1, "Only E and 7 could reveal a vowel paired with an odd number; 4 cannot break the rule."],
    [2, "Why is the drinking-age version of the card puzzle so interesting?", ["Same logic, yet people solve it far more easily","It uses a completely different kind of logic","Hardly anyone ever solves it correctly","It shows people are better at maths when relaxed"], 0, "The logic is the same as E and 7, but a familiar setting makes the cases that could break the rule obvious."],
    [3, "What is confirmation bias?", ["Changing your mind whenever someone disagrees","Trusting only what recognised experts say","Avoiding strong opinions on every subject","Favouring information that backs your existing beliefs"], 3, "We search for, interpret and remember evidence in ways that back up our existing views."],
    [4, "According to the 1984 study by Lord and colleagues, what helped reduce biased judgements?", ["Being told to be fair","Reading more evidence that supports your view","Deliberately considering the opposite","Deciding more quickly"], 2, "Actively imagining you could be wrong works better than simply promising to be unbiased."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Three numbers and a hidden rule. Almost everyone makes the same mistake."});

WB.L("outcome-bias", "thinking", "beginner", "Outcome bias: why good decisions can end badly",
  "A coach made one of the most criticised calls in sport, and a poker player says we judged it all wrong. Learn to tell a smart choice from a lucky one.",
  [
    ["The worst call ever?", "Judging a decision only by how it turned out, which poker players call ‘resulting’, is a mistake.",
      "In February 2015 the Seattle Seahawks were seconds from winning the Super Bowl. Trailing the New England Patriots by four points, they had the ball about a metre from the line with 26 seconds left. Most fans expected a handoff to their powerful running back. Instead, coach Pete Carroll called a pass. It was intercepted, and the Patriots won. Headlines called it one of the worst calls in the game's history. Yet some analysts argued it was reasonable: interceptions that close to the line are rare, and an incomplete pass would stop the clock. Former poker player Annie Duke, in her 2018 book Thinking in Bets, calls judging by results alone ‘resulting’. In poker you can play a hand perfectly and still lose to the last card. In life there are fewer hands and messier information."],
    ["The surgeon experiment", "People rate the very same decision as worse, and the decision-maker as less competent, when it turns out badly.",
      "In 1988 the psychologists Jonathan Baron and John Hershey gave volunteers descriptions of decisions made under uncertainty, such as a doctor's choice about whether to go ahead with a risky operation. Everyone received the same information that had been available to the decision-maker at the time. The only thing that changed was the outcome: in some versions the patient lived, and in others the patient died. People rated the decision itself as worse, and the decision-maker as less competent, when the outcome was bad. Yet the choice had been made with exactly the same knowledge. Baron and Hershey called this outcome bias."],
    ["Four kinds of result", "Every outcome mixes decision quality with luck, and a bad decision that works out well is the most dangerous combination.",
      "Picture a simple grid. A good decision with a good outcome is a deserved success. A good decision with a bad outcome is bad luck. A bad decision with a bad outcome is a predictable failure. And a bad decision with a good outcome is a lucky escape, which may be the most dangerous of all. Think of someone who drives home after too many drinks and arrives safely. The outcome teaches them nothing useful, or worse, teaches them it was fine. Each lucky escape can make the next risky choice feel safer. Outcome bias collapses these four boxes into two: it worked, so it was right; it failed, so it was wrong."],
    ["Hindsight makes it worse", "Once we know how things turned out, we overestimate how predictable they were.",
      "Outcome bias has a close relative: hindsight bias, the feeling that we ‘knew it all along’. In the 1970s the psychologist Baruch Fischhoff showed that once people learnt how an event turned out, they judged that outcome to have been more predictable than people who hadn't been told. Their memories of what they had predicted earlier also shifted towards what actually happened. Together these biases make the world seem more orderly than it really is. They also make us unfair judges. Workers, doctors, pilots and leaders can be blamed for bad outcomes they could not reasonably have foreseen, while people who took foolish risks and got away with them can be rewarded and promoted."],
    ["Judge the process", "To learn from experience, review how a decision was made, not just how it turned out.",
      "The fix is to judge decisions by the process behind them. Ask: what did we know at the time? What options did we consider? Did we think about the risks and how likely they were? Was there information we could reasonably have gathered but didn't? Armies, hospitals and airlines have long used structured reviews after events, and these work best when they examine successes and failures with the same honesty. A simple personal tool is a decision journal. Before an important choice, write down what you expect to happen and why. Later, compare that with what actually happened. Over time you'll see whether your judgement is sound, or whether you've simply been lucky."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does ‘resulting’ mean?", ["Recording the score after each game","Judging a choice only by its result","Making decisions much too quickly","Betting only on the likely winners"], 1, "Resulting ignores luck and judges the choice by its outcome alone."],
    [1, "In Baron and Hershey's study, what changed between versions of each scenario?", ["The information available to the doctor","The doctor's experience","The type of operation","Only the outcome"], 3, "With identical information, a bad outcome alone made people judge the decision and the decision-maker more harshly."],
    [2, "Why might a bad decision with a good outcome be especially dangerous?", ["It always leads to a later punishment","It is impossible for anyone to notice","It can teach people a risky choice was fine","It makes people far too cautious"], 2, "A lucky escape rewards the risk, making the next risky choice feel safer."],
    [3, "What is hindsight bias?", ["Forgetting which decisions you made last year","Thinking an outcome was obvious once you know it","Planning so far ahead that plans stop working","Trusting experts less after one mistake"], 1, "Knowing the result makes it seem obvious, so we judge past decisions unfairly."],
    [4, "What is a good way to learn from a decision?", ["Review what you knew and what you weighed","Judge it only by how it turned out","Avoid reviewing decisions that worked","Blame whoever was in charge at the time"], 0, "Reviewing the process, for example with a decision journal, separates skill from luck."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A coach made the most criticised call in sport. Was it actually a bad decision?"});

WB.L("n-rays-blondlot", "thinking", "beginner", "N-rays: the rays only believers could see",
  "In 1903 a respected French physicist discovered a new kind of radiation, and other scientists soon confirmed it. There was just one problem: it didn't exist.",
  [
    ["An age of invisible rays", "After X-rays and radioactivity, physicists were primed to believe new rays existed, and N-rays were judged by eye.",
      "At the end of the 1800s physics was full of astonishing discoveries. In 1895 Wilhelm Röntgen found X-rays. The next year Henri Becquerel found that uranium gave off its own radiation, and soon Marie and Pierre Curie were isolating new radioactive elements. In 1903 René Blondlot, a respected physicist at the University of Nancy, announced another kind, which he named N-rays after his home city. The claim seemed like the next exciting chapter. N-rays supposedly made a small spark or a faintly glowing screen grow slightly brighter, and observers judged this by eye in a darkened room. That is a very weak signal: in dim light the eye struggles to judge small differences in brightness, and tiny flickers are easy to read as meaningful. Still, other scientists reported observations of their own."],
    ["Others couldn't see them", "When a result can only be seen by believers, and failures are blamed on the observer, the claim is very hard to test.",
      "Not everyone was convinced. Physicists in Germany and Britain, among them some of the most skilled experimenters of the day, set up the apparatus and saw nothing. Supporters had an answer ready: perhaps the sceptics' eyes were not sensitive enough, or they lacked the right technique. This is a warning sign worth remembering. When a result can only be seen by people who already believe in it, and failures are explained away as the observer's fault, the claim has become very difficult to test. A real effect should show up for careful researchers whether or not they hope to find it. The disagreement rumbled on, until a visiting American physicist decided to investigate for himself."],
    ["The missing prism", "In 1904 Robert Wood secretly removed a vital part of the equipment, and the readings carried on.",
      "In 1904 the American physicist Robert W. Wood, of Johns Hopkins University, visited Blondlot's laboratory. Blondlot demonstrated how an aluminium prism supposedly spread N-rays into a spectrum, with readings taken in a darkened room. While the room was dark, Wood quietly removed the prism and kept it out of sight. Blondlot carried on reading out measurements as if nothing had changed. Wood also reported other secret checks in which observers kept seeing effects that should have vanished. He published his account in the journal Nature later that year. Without accusing anyone of dishonesty, the report made it clear that the observations depended on what the observers expected, not on any real rays."],
    ["Self-deception, not fraud", "Blondlot almost certainly believed what he saw, because expectation shapes perception.",
      "There is no good evidence that Blondlot set out to deceive anyone. He was an accomplished scientist who had done respected earlier work measuring the speed of electrical waves. What went wrong was more ordinary and more unsettling: he and others genuinely perceived what they expected to perceive. After Wood's report, interest in N-rays faded quickly, especially outside France, though Blondlot is said to have kept believing in them. The episode is now a classic case study in how expectation shapes observation. The chemist Irving Langmuir later popularised the phrase ‘pathological science’ for cases like this, where researchers fool themselves with effects so faint they sit right at the edge of what can be detected."],
    ["Clever Hans and blinding", "Good science guards against expectation by keeping observers from knowing what to expect.",
      "A similar lesson came from a horse. In early 1900s Berlin, a horse called Clever Hans appeared to answer arithmetic questions by tapping his hoof. In 1907 the psychologist Oskar Pfungst showed that Hans was responding to tiny, unconscious cues from the people around him, who tensed and relaxed as he neared the right answer. When the questioner didn't know the answer, or Hans couldn't see them, his skill vanished. The solution to both stories is blinding. In a well-run modern trial, the people measuring results don't know which participants received the treatment. Wood, in effect, ran a blind test. Whenever you can, test a claim in a way that stops the tester's hopes from steering the result."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did N-rays seem believable in 1903?", ["They were bright enough to see in daylight","They had been predicted decades earlier","Recent finds such as X-rays made new rays plausible","Every laboratory in Europe had detected them"], 2, "Recent discoveries of invisible rays primed physicists to accept one more kind."],
    [1, "What warning sign appeared when other laboratories couldn't find N-rays?", ["The sceptics refused to try the experiment","The equipment was too expensive to build","The sceptics were not trained physicists","Failures were blamed on the sceptics' eyes"], 3, "A claim that only believers can confirm, with failures excused, is very hard to test."],
    [2, "What happened when Robert Wood secretly removed the prism?", ["The readings stopped at once","The readings carried on as before","Blondlot spotted the missing prism","The rays seemed to grow stronger"], 1, "Readings that continue without the key part show the observations depended on expectation."],
    [3, "How did Blondlot come to ‘see’ rays that weren't there?", ["He deliberately forged the readings","His expectations shaped what he sincerely saw","His instruments gave faulty readings","He copied results from other laboratories"], 1, "Expectation shaped observation, so he sincerely saw what he hoped to see."],
    [4, "What does ‘blinding’ mean in an experiment?", ["Running the experiment in complete darkness","Keeping the final results from the public","Testing only on animals, never people","Keeping testers unaware of key details"], 3, "If testers don't know what to expect, their hopes can't steer the result."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A respected scientist found rays that other labs couldn't. A visitor spotted why."});

WB.L("deduction-induction-abduction", "thinking", "beginner", "Deduction, induction and abduction: three ways to reason",
  "Sherlock Holmes called it deduction, but he was usually doing something else. Learn the three basic kinds of reasoning, and what each can and can't prove.",
  [
    ["Holmes reads Watson", "Holmes's first meeting with Watson shows reasoning from small clues to a big conclusion, though not strictly deduction.",
      "In A Study in Scarlet, published in 1887, Sherlock Holmes shakes hands with Dr John Watson and says, ‘You have been in Afghanistan, I perceive.’ Watson is astonished. Holmes later explains his chain of thought. Here was a gentleman of a medical type with a military air, so probably an army doctor. His face was tanned but his wrists were pale, so he had recently come from the tropics. He looked haggard and held his left arm stiffly, suggesting illness and an injury. Where might an English army doctor recently have suffered such hardship? Afghanistan. Holmes called this the science of deduction. Logicians would say he was mostly doing something different. To see why, we need three kinds of reasoning."],
    ["Deduction: certainty", "In a valid deduction, if the premises are true, the conclusion must be true.",
      "Deduction moves from general statements to a conclusion that follows necessarily. All humans are mortal; Socrates is human; therefore Socrates is mortal. If both starting statements, called premises, are true, the conclusion cannot be false. That is deduction's great strength: certainty. Its weakness is that the conclusion contains nothing that wasn't already packed inside the premises. Deduction also depends completely on the premises being right. An argument can be valid in structure and still reach a false conclusion if a premise is wrong: all birds can fly; penguins are birds; therefore penguins can fly. Logicians call an argument ‘sound’ only when it is valid and its premises are actually true."],
    ["Induction: patterns", "Induction generalises from experience, which is useful but never completely certain.",
      "Induction works the other way, from particular observations to general conclusions. The sun has risen every morning in recorded history, so it will rise tomorrow. Every swan Europeans had ever seen was white, so all swans must be white. Most of our everyday knowledge, and much of science, rests on reasoning like this, and usually it serves us well. But an inductive conclusion can always be overturned by new evidence. In 1697 the Dutch explorer Willem de Vlamingh and his crew saw black swans on a river in what is now Western Australia, which became known as the Swan River. A belief backed by countless observations was undone by a single flock of birds. Induction also fails when we generalise from too few cases, such as judging a whole city on one rude waiter."],
    ["Abduction: best explanation", "Abduction reasons from evidence to the most likely explanation, but it can miss better alternatives.",
      "The American philosopher Charles Sanders Peirce named a third kind, abduction. It starts with a surprising observation and asks what would best explain it. The lawn is wet this morning. Did it rain, did the sprinkler run, or did dew form? A bone-dry footpath points to the sprinkler. This is also called inference to the best explanation, and it is what Holmes was really doing with Watson. Doctors diagnosing from symptoms and mechanics tracing an engine noise do the same. It is risky, because the best explanation you can think of may not be true: perhaps Watson was tanned from a holiday and hurt his arm falling off a horse. Treating abduction as deduction is a known fallacy, affirming the consequent: ‘If it rained, the lawn would be wet. The lawn is wet. Therefore it rained.’"],
    ["Using all three together", "Good thinking cycles through explanation, prediction and testing.",
      "In practice the three kinds of reasoning work as a team, and science is a good model. A researcher notices something odd and uses abduction to propose a likely explanation, called a hypothesis. They then use deduction to work out what should happen if that explanation is true: if this fertiliser helps plants grow, then plants given it should grow taller than identical plants without it. Finally they gather observations and use induction to judge whether the evidence supports the idea. Then the cycle starts again. You can do the same with everyday puzzles. When you land on an explanation, ask what else could explain the facts, what your idea predicts, and whether you've checked."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which kind of reasoning was Holmes mostly using when he guessed Watson had been in Afghanistan?", ["Deduction","Abduction","Induction","Pure intuition"], 1, "Holmes chose the most plausible explanation for the clues, which is abduction."],
    [1, "What makes a deductive argument ‘sound’?", ["It is convincing to most people","It is based on many observations","It has a surprising conclusion","It is valid and its premises are true"], 3, "A valid structure only guarantees a true conclusion when the premises are true as well."],
    [2, "What did the black swans seen in 1697 show about induction?", ["Strong evidence can still be overturned","Induction always leads to false conclusions","Swans migrate to Australia each year","Deduction is more reliable than observation"], 0, "Induction is useful but never certain, because new evidence can always overturn it."],
    [3, "‘If it rained, the lawn would be wet. The lawn is wet, so it rained.’ What's wrong with this?", ["Nothing, it is a perfectly valid deduction","It wrongly assumes that rain is rare","It treats one possible cause as the only one","It relies on far too few observations"], 2, "A wet lawn has other possible explanations, so the conclusion is only a guess at the best one."],
    [4, "In the scientific cycle, what role does deduction usually play?", ["Coming up with the first explanation","Predicting what follows if the idea is true","Collecting and counting observations","Making further testing unnecessary"], 1, "Deduction turns a hypothesis into a testable prediction."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Sherlock Holmes called it deduction. Logicians say he was doing something else."});

WB.L("framing-effect", "thinking", "intermediate", "The framing effect: same facts, different choices",
  "Would you choose a treatment with a 90 per cent survival rate? What about one with a 10 per cent death rate? They're identical, yet the wording changes what people choose.",
  [
    ["An imaginary outbreak", "Describing identical options as lives saved or lives lost flips people's choices.",
      "In 1981 the psychologists Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman published a now-famous problem. Imagine a disease outbreak expected to kill 600 people. One group chose between two programs. With Program A, 200 people will be saved. With Program B, there's a one-third chance all 600 are saved and a two-thirds chance no-one is saved. Most chose A, the sure thing. A second group got the same choice in different words. With Program C, 400 people will die. With Program D, there's a one-third chance nobody dies and a two-thirds chance all 600 die. Now most chose D, the gamble. Yet A and C describe exactly the same outcome, as do B and D."],
    ["Gains safe, losses risky", "People avoid risk when outcomes are framed as gains, and gamble to avoid sure losses.",
      "The outbreak problem revealed a pattern that Kahneman and Tversky built into their prospect theory. When outcomes are described as gains, such as lives saved, people tend to be cautious and prefer a certain result. When the same outcomes are described as losses, such as deaths, people become more willing to gamble to avoid a sure loss. Part of the reason is loss aversion: losses tend to feel more painful than equivalent gains feel good, with many studies putting the ratio at roughly two to one, though estimates vary. Logically, the wording should make no difference. Psychologically, the frame sets the reference point against which everything else is judged."],
    ["Doctors are not immune", "Even physicians' choices shifted depending on whether outcomes were framed as survival or death.",
      "You might hope experts would see through framing. In a 1982 study in the New England Journal of Medicine, Barbara McNeil and colleagues, including Tversky, asked people to choose between surgery and radiation therapy for lung cancer. Surgery carried a higher risk of dying during or soon after treatment, but better long-term survival. When the results were presented as chances of surviving, surgery looked more attractive than when the very same results were described as chances of dying. The effect appeared among patients, among students, and among physicians. Since then, many guides to communicating medical risk have encouraged presenting figures in more than one way, for example giving both survival and death rates together."],
    ["Framing in shops and news", "Highlighting the positive side of the same facts, or one aspect of an issue, shifts judgements and opinions.",
      "In a 1988 study, Irwin Levin and Gary Gaeth asked people to evaluate minced beef described as either ‘75 per cent lean’ or ‘25 per cent fat’. Same meat, but the ‘lean’ version was rated better quality and less greasy. The gap shrank when people tasted the beef, though it didn't vanish. Yoghurt is ‘95 per cent fat free’ rather than ‘5 per cent fat’. A fee is a ‘discount for paying cash’ rather than a ‘surcharge for paying by card’. A broader kind of framing highlights different aspects of an issue. In a 1997 study, readers of a news story about a planned Ku Klux Klan rally were more tolerant of it when it was framed as free speech than as a threat to public order. Is a policy ‘tax relief’ or a ‘cut to services’?"],
    ["Defusing the frame", "Restate choices in more than one way and convert percentages into real numbers before deciding.",
      "You can't avoid frames, because every description has to start somewhere. But you can learn to notice them. When you face an important choice, deliberately flip the wording: if you're told nine out of ten customers are satisfied, remind yourself that one in ten isn't. If a deal is framed as avoiding a loss, ask how it looks as a plain gain. Turn percentages into concrete counts, such as ‘out of 100 people like me, how many?’ This makes risks much easier to compare. Be especially wary when someone else has chosen the frame for you and stands to gain from your decision. And when you present information to others, giving both frames is often the fairest thing you can do."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "In the outbreak problem, why is the switch in people's choices surprising?", ["The programs had genuinely different outcomes","The second group was given more information","Programs A and C described exactly the same outcome","The participants were all doctors"], 2, "Only the wording changed, from lives saved to lives lost, yet the choices flipped."],
    [1, "When outcomes are framed as losses, people tend to:", ["Become more cautious","Become more willing to gamble","Choose whichever option is listed first","Ignore the numbers entirely"], 1, "Because losses feel painful, people will take a risk to avoid a sure one."],
    [2, "Who was influenced by framing in the 1982 lung cancer study?", ["Only students","Only patients without medical training","Patients, students and physicians alike","Nobody, once the numbers were explained"], 2, "Medical expertise did not protect against framing, so presenting figures both ways is wise."],
    [3, "What happened in the minced beef study when people actually tasted the meat?", ["The effect became much stronger","The effect shrank but did not vanish","People preferred the ‘fat’ label","Tasting made them reject both samples"], 1, "Direct experience reduced the effect, but the label still coloured the judgement."],
    [4, "Which is a good way to defuse a frame?", ["Go with your first gut reaction every time","Trust only the positive descriptions","Avoid numbers and rely on instinct","Flip the wording and use real counts"], 3, "Flipping the wording and using real counts reveals what the frame was hiding."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Ninety per cent survival and ten per cent death mean the same. Why do choices differ?"});

WB.L("wisdom-of-crowds", "thinking", "intermediate", "The wisdom of crowds: when many guesses beat experts",
  "In 1906 hundreds of people guessed the weight of an ox. Hardly anyone was spot on, but together they were astonishingly close. Here's why, and when crowds turn foolish.",
  [
    ["The ox at Plymouth", "A crowd's middle guess of an ox's weight in 1906 came within about one per cent of the truth.",
      "In 1906 the scientist Francis Galton visited a livestock fair in Plymouth, England. Visitors could pay a small fee to guess the weight of an ox once it had been slaughtered and dressed, with prizes for the closest entries. Galton, who was not a great admirer of ordinary people's judgement, collected the 787 legible tickets and analysed them. The guesses were all over the place. But the middle value, the median, was 1,207 pounds, about 547 kilograms. The actual weight was 1,198 pounds, so the crowd was out by less than one per cent. Later analyses found the average was even closer. Galton published the result in Nature in 1907 under the title ‘Vox Populi’, the voice of the people."],
    ["Why errors cancel out", "When people's mistakes are independent, overestimates and underestimates tend to cancel, so the average beats most individuals.",
      "How can a crowd of non-experts beat almost every individual in it? Each guess contains some genuine information and some error. One person overestimates because the ox looks bulky; another underestimates because they're thinking of a smaller breed. If those errors point in different directions, they cancel out when averaged, while the useful information adds up. The result is often better than most individual guesses, and sometimes better than an expert's. James Surowiecki popularised the idea in his 2004 book The Wisdom of Crowds. By his account, on Who Wants to Be a Millionaire? the contestants' chosen friends gave the right answer about 65 per cent of the time, while the studio audience's most popular answer was right about 91 per cent of the time."],
    ["Four conditions", "Crowds are wise when they are diverse, independent, decentralised and properly combined.",
      "Surowiecki argued that wise crowds need four ingredients. Diversity: people should bring different information and perspectives, not all the same views. Independence: each person's judgement should be their own, rather than copied from those around them. Decentralisation: people should be able to draw on local and specialised knowledge. And aggregation: there must be some way of combining all the private judgements into one answer, such as an average, a vote or a market price. Take away any of these and the magic fades. The same principles underpin tools from averaged economic forecasts to pooled product ratings. A room of people who all read the same article, or who call out their guesses one after another, is less a crowd of independent minds than one opinion repeated many times."],
    ["When crowds go wrong", "Social influence can make a crowd more confident without making it more accurate.",
      "Independence is the fragile ingredient. In a 2011 study, Jan Lorenz and colleagues in Switzerland asked groups to estimate factual quantities, such as crime figures, over several rounds. Some groups were shown what the others had guessed before answering again. Seeing others' answers narrowed the range of guesses and made people more confident, but the group's estimates did not become more accurate. The crowd had lost its diversity without gaining any wisdom. On a larger scale, similar dynamics help explain stock market bubbles, fads and panic buying, where people react to each other rather than to independent information. A crowd that copies itself doesn't cancel out its errors. It can amplify them."],
    ["The crowd inside you", "Averaging two separate guesses of your own can be more accurate than either one, especially if you wait between them.",
      "You can even borrow the effect for yourself. In 2008 Edward Vul and Harold Pashler asked people to answer factual questions, then unexpectedly asked them to guess again. The average of a person's two guesses was more accurate than either guess alone, and the benefit was larger when the second guess came three weeks later rather than straight away. The delay helped because it made the second guess more independent of the first. Researchers call this the crowd within. A practical version: when you estimate something important, such as how long a job will take, make a guess, then assume your first answer was wrong, think about why, and make a second estimate. Then average the two."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was striking about the guesses at the Plymouth fair?", ["Most individuals guessed almost exactly right","The butchers' guesses were by far the best","Nobody guessed within 100 pounds of the weight","The crowd's median was about one per cent out"], 3, "Individual guesses were scattered, yet the crowd's middle value was remarkably accurate."],
    [1, "Why does averaging many guesses work?", ["Independent errors tend to cancel out","Crowds always contain an expert","People guess more carefully in groups","Large numbers are easier to estimate"], 0, "Overestimates and underestimates offset each other while the real information adds up."],
    [2, "Which of these is NOT one of Surowiecki's conditions for a wise crowd?", ["Diversity","Independence","Unanimity","Aggregation"], 2, "Wise crowds need diverse, independent views that are combined, not everyone agreeing."],
    [3, "What happened when people in Lorenz's study saw others' guesses?", ["Accuracy improved sharply as guesses converged","Guesses spread out much more widely than before","Most people refused to guess a second time","Guesses narrowed, but accuracy did not improve"], 3, "Copying reduces diversity and independence, so the crowd gets surer without getting wiser."],
    [4, "Why did a second guess made weeks later help more than an immediate one?", ["People simply remembered the answer better","The second guess was more independent","The first guess was nearly always wrong","Waiting made people far more confident"], 1, "Independence is what lets errors cancel, and a delay supplies some of it."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Hundreds of people guessed an ox's weight. Why the crowd beat almost every individual."});

WB.L("law-of-small-numbers", "thinking", "intermediate", "Small samples, big surprises: the law of small numbers",
  "The places with the lowest cancer rates and the highest ones share the same surprising feature. Understanding why will change how you read every ranking and review.",
  [
    ["A puzzle about cancer maps", "The places that look healthiest and unhealthiest are often the least populated, because small samples swing to extremes.",
      "Imagine a map of the United States showing kidney cancer rates by county. The counties with the lowest rates are mostly rural and sparsely populated. Fresh air, clean water, active lives? Now look at the counties with the highest rates. They are also mostly rural and sparsely populated. Rural life can't be both protective and harmful. The statistician Howard Wainer, and later Daniel Kahneman in Thinking, Fast and Slow, explained why: small counties have small populations, and small numbers bounce around far more. Toss a fair coin four times and all heads happens about one time in sixteen. Toss it 400 times and it is practically impossible. Small samples crowd both the top and the bottom of any ranking."],
    ["The hospital question", "Even people trained in statistics tend to ignore sample size when it matters.",
      "In the early 1970s Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman posed a question. A town has two hospitals. In the larger one about 45 babies are born each day, and in the smaller one about 15. Roughly half of all babies are boys, but the exact share varies from day to day. Over a year, each hospital records the days when more than 60 per cent of the babies born were boys. Which hospital recorded more such days? Most people said about the same. The right answer is the smaller hospital, because with only 15 births a day, lopsided results are much more common. In an earlier paper titled ‘Belief in the law of small numbers’, they had shown that even research psychologists trusted small samples too much."],
    ["The most dangerous equation", "To halve the uncertainty in an average, you need four times as much data.",
      "The maths traces back to the 1700s and the French-born mathematician Abraham de Moivre. In modern terms, the typical wobble in an average shrinks in proportion to the square root of the number of things being averaged. That has a sobering consequence. To halve the uncertainty in an estimate, you don't need twice as much data; you need four times as much. To cut it to a tenth, you need a hundred times more. Howard Wainer has called this ‘the most dangerous equation’, not because it's complicated, but because ignoring it has led to so many costly mistakes. It explains why tiny trials, small surveys and short track records can be so misleading."],
    ["The small schools mistake", "Policies built on small-sample rankings can end up chasing noise.",
      "Wainer's favourite cautionary tale involves American education. From the late 1990s, reformers noticed that small schools were overrepresented among the top performers on test results. Major funders, including the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation, invested heavily in creating smaller schools and breaking up big ones. But as Wainer and colleagues pointed out, small schools were also overrepresented among the worst performers. Their results were simply more variable, because each year's scores came from fewer students. Evaluations of the small-schools push produced mixed results, and the Gates Foundation later shifted its education strategy. Whatever the other merits of smaller schools, the striking rankings had been at least partly an illusion created by sample size."],
    ["Reading rankings wisely", "Before trusting an extreme result, check how many cases it is based on.",
      "Once you know this, you'll see it everywhere. A new café with five reviews averaging five stars may be no better than an established one averaging 4.6 from 2,000 reviews. A footballer who scores in each of their first three games isn't necessarily a star. A small study reporting a dramatic effect is more likely to be a fluke than a large study reporting a modest one. A useful habit is to mentally pull extreme results from small samples back towards the average until more evidence comes in. Statisticians have formal versions of this idea, sometimes called shrinkage. And when you hear that a small town is the best or worst place for something, your first question should be: how many people live there?"]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do US counties with the lowest and the highest kidney cancer rates often share small populations?", ["Small counties are close to large cities","Small populations swing to extremes by chance","Small counties share the same water supply","Rural life is both protective and harmful"], 1, "Small samples swing widely, so they fill both ends of a ranking."],
    [1, "In the hospital problem, which hospital records more days when over 60 per cent of babies are boys?", ["The larger hospital","Both about the same","It depends on the season","The smaller hospital"], 3, "With fewer births per day, lopsided results are much more common."],
    [2, "To halve the uncertainty in an average, roughly how much more data do you need?", ["Twice as much","Ten times as much","Four times as much","Half as much"], 2, "Uncertainty shrinks with the square root of the sample size, so halving it takes four times the data."],
    [3, "Why were small schools overrepresented among the top performers?", ["Fewer students made their results swing more","Their teachers were consistently better","They received far more funding per pupil","They were given much easier tests"], 0, "The same variability also put small schools among the worst performers, so the pattern was largely noise."],
    [4, "A new café has five reviews, all five stars. What's the most sensible view?", ["It's definitely the best café in town","Five-star ratings are usually fake","Too few reviews to judge it with confidence","Ignore all online reviews from now on"], 2, "Extreme results from small samples should be tempered until more evidence arrives."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The healthiest and unhealthiest places on the map share one odd feature."});

WB.L("piltdown-man-hoax", "thinking", "intermediate", "Piltdown Man: the fake fossil that fooled science for 40 years",
  "For four decades, a skull from an English gravel pit was hailed as a key link in human evolution. It was a forgery, and the reasons experts believed it are the real lesson.",
  [
    ["A discovery in Sussex", "In 1912 bones from a Sussex gravel pit were announced as a vital human ancestor.",
      "In December 1912 Charles Dawson, a solicitor and keen amateur fossil collector, and Arthur Smith Woodward, a leading geologist at the British Museum (Natural History), presented a sensational find to the Geological Society of London. From a gravel pit near Piltdown in Sussex came fragments of a skull and part of a jawbone with two worn teeth, along with animal fossils and crude stone tools. Woodward's reconstruction showed a creature with a large, humanlike braincase and an apelike jaw. It was named Eoanthropus dawsoni, ‘Dawson's dawn man’. Newspapers celebrated the earliest Englishman, and many experts accepted Piltdown as a vital link between apes and humans."],
    ["Expectation and missing evidence", "Piltdown told experts what they wanted to hear, and the evidence that could have exposed it was missing or hard to reach.",
      "Piltdown confirmed what many scientists already thought: that a large brain evolved before other human features. National pride played a part too. Germany had the Neanderthal remains and the Heidelberg jaw, and France had Cro-Magnon skeletons, but Britain had little of comparable importance. There were warning signs. The jaw was broken exactly where it would have shown whether it belonged with the skull: the joint and the chin were missing. Some scientists, especially in the United States and France, argued early that skull and jaw came from different creatures. But most researchers saw only plaster casts, which could not show tool marks or staining. Later finds, including a canine tooth in 1913, seemed to settle the debate."],
    ["A real ancestor dismissed", "Because Piltdown seemed authentic, a genuine African fossil was sidelined for decades.",
      "The forgery did real damage. In 1924 the Australian-born anatomist Raymond Dart, working in South Africa, identified a small fossil skull from a quarry at Taung. In 1925 he argued in Nature that this ‘Taung Child’ belonged to an early human relative, which he named Australopithecus africanus. It had the opposite combination to Piltdown: a small, apelike brain but humanlike teeth and signs of upright posture. Many leading experts dismissed it, partly because it clashed with Piltdown's big-brain-first story. It took more than two decades, and many further fossil discoveries in Africa, before Dart's view was widely accepted. We now know that upright walking came long before large brains."],
    ["Exposed in 1953", "New dating methods and careful examination proved the bones were a recent fake, probably by Dawson.",
      "As genuine fossils emerged, Piltdown looked out of place. In 1949 Kenneth Oakley applied a new fluorine test, which helps estimate how long bones have been buried. The Piltdown bones contained surprisingly little fluorine, suggesting they were far younger than claimed. In 1953 Oakley, with Joseph Weiner and Wilfrid Le Gros Clark, announced the truth. The jaw came from an orangutan, its teeth filed down to look humanlike. The skull belonged to a modern human who had lived only centuries earlier. The bones had been stained to look ancient. For decades people argued over who did it, with suspects including Woodward and even Arthur Conan Doyle. A 2016 study using DNA analysis and CT scanning pointed to a single forger, and Dawson was by far the most likely."],
    ["Lessons from a forgery", "Be most careful with evidence that tells you exactly what you hoped to hear.",
      "Piltdown wasn't exposed because science is infallible; it was exposed because science keeps testing. Better methods and new evidence eventually revealed what expectation had hidden. But the story offers lessons well beyond fossils. Be extra careful with evidence that confirms exactly what you, your team or your country hoped to find. Ask whether anyone has examined the original data, rather than a summary or a copy. Notice when crucial pieces of evidence are conveniently missing. And remember that the anomaly that doesn't fit, like the Taung Child, may turn out to be the real discovery. When an idea seems to explain everything you expected, that's a moment to look harder, not to relax."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Woodward's reconstruction of Piltdown Man show?", ["A small brain with a humanlike jaw","A large, humanlike braincase with an apelike jaw","A complete skeleton walking upright","A skull identical to a Neanderthal"], 1, "This big-brain, ape-jaw combination looked like a missing link."],
    [1, "Why were most researchers unable to spot the forgery early on?", ["The original fossils were lost for decades","Most only saw plaster casts, not the originals","The bones were locked in a foreign museum","No-one was allowed to publish about them"], 1, "Casts hid tool marks and staining, and the missing joint and chin hid the mismatch."],
    [2, "What was Raymond Dart's Taung Child?", ["Another forgery by the same hoaxer","A Neanderthal skull from South Africa","A real early human relative, long dismissed","An orangutan skull with filed teeth"], 2, "Its small brain and humanlike teeth clashed with Piltdown's story, so a real ancestor was sidelined."],
    [3, "What did Oakley's fluorine test reveal?", ["The bones were millions of years old","The bones came from Africa","The skull belonged to an ape","The bones were far younger than claimed"], 3, "Low fluorine showed the bones had not been buried for long, which began the unmasking."],
    [4, "What is the main lesson of Piltdown for everyday thinking?", ["Never trust the word of any scientist","Be extra wary of evidence you were hoping for","Fossils found in gravel pits are usually fakes","Trust summaries over the original data"], 1, "Convenient evidence that flatters our expectations deserves more scrutiny, not less."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"For 40 years a fossil fooled experts. What they wanted to believe was the real trap."});

WB.L("lady-tasting-tea", "thinking", "advanced", "The lady tasting tea: how to design a fair test",
  "A scientist claimed she could taste whether the milk or the tea went into the cup first. Working out how to test her claim properly helped shape the modern experiment.",
  [
    ["Tea at Rothamsted", "A casual claim at afternoon tea led Ronald Fisher to ask how to test any claim fairly.",
      "In the early 1920s, at the Rothamsted agricultural research station north of London, the statistician Ronald Fisher offered a colleague a cup of tea. Dr Muriel Bristol, a scientist who studied algae, declined because he had poured the milk in after the tea. She preferred milk first, and insisted she could taste the difference.\n\nFisher was sceptical. According to later accounts from people who were there, a test was quickly arranged, and she reportedly identified the cups correctly. Fisher used the story to open a chapter of his 1935 book The Design of Experiments. Behind a light-hearted example lay a serious question: how do you fairly test a claim?"],
    ["What would luck look like?", "A fair test starts by assuming pure guessing, the null hypothesis, and working out how often guessing would succeed.",
      "Fisher's key move was to assume the claim is false. Suppose Bristol has no ability and is simply guessing. This assumption is now called the null hypothesis. Then ask whether her actual performance would be surprising by comparison.\n\nHis design used eight cups, four milk-first and four tea-first, in random order. She would pick out the four milk-first cups. There are 70 ways to choose four cups from eight, and only one is fully correct. A guesser has a 1 in 70 chance of a perfect score, about 1.4 per cent. Three right and one wrong can happen 16 ways, so scoring at least three out of four by luck is 17 in 70, about 24 per cent. That's too common to prove anything. Decide what counts as convincing before you run the test."],
    ["Why randomise?", "Random order stops hidden patterns or clues from being mistaken for skill.",
      "Why serve the cups in random order? Imagine the four milk-first cups were all prepared and served first. They might be slightly cooler, or the taster might notice a pattern. Any success could then come from those clues rather than from tasting the milk.\n\nFisher argued that randomisation is the most reliable protection against hidden factors like these, including ones nobody has thought of. He also stressed keeping everything else the same: the same cups, the same tea and the same amount of milk. He developed these ideas through crop experiments at Rothamsted, where treatments were assigned to plots of land at random so that differences in soil couldn't bias the results."],
    ["Size matters", "A test that is too small can never give convincing evidence, however genuine the skill.",
      "Fisher's framework also shows why the number of trials matters. With six cups, three of each, there are only 20 ways to choose, so even a perfect score has a 1 in 20 chance of happening by luck, or five per cent. With just four cups, two of each, a guesser gets a perfect score one time in six. A test that small could never convince anyone, however good the taster.\n\nFisher suggested treating results that would happen by chance less than about one time in 20 as worth taking seriously. That convention later hardened into the famous 0.05 threshold for statistical significance. He saw it as a rough guide to which results deserve attention, not a magic line between truth and falsehood."],
    ["Trials, and a warning", "Fisher's principles underpin modern trials and A/B tests, but a good method doesn't cure wishful thinking.",
      "Fisher's ideas now run through modern research. Drug trials assign patients to treatment or placebo at random and ask how likely the results would be if the drug did nothing. Farmers test crop varieties in randomised plots. Technology companies run A/B tests, randomly showing different web page versions to different users. Three questions travel well. What would I expect to see if this claim were false? Could something else explain the result? Was the test big enough to tell?\n\nYet Fisher himself erred. In the late 1950s he argued that the smoking and lung cancer link might not be causal, suggesting an inherited factor could explain both. Asking about hidden causes is legitimate, but the evidence against smoking became overwhelming. Fisher was a keen pipe smoker and had consulted for the tobacco industry."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What question did the tea story lead Fisher to explore?", ["How to fairly test whether a claim is true","How to brew tea consistently","Whether milk affects the taste of algae","How to measure a taster's experience"], 0, "The light-hearted example opened a serious inquiry into how to test any claim fairly."],
    [1, "What is the null hypothesis in the tea test?", ["That she can always tell the difference","That milk-first tea tastes better","That she has no ability and is simply guessing","That the cups were poured incorrectly"], 2, "A fair test starts by assuming guessing, then asks whether the result would be surprising under that assumption."],
    [2, "Why should the cups be served in random order?", ["To make the test quicker to run","So hidden patterns can't pass for skill","To reduce the number of cups needed","So the taster drinks less tea overall"], 1, "Randomisation protects against hidden factors, including ones nobody has thought of."],
    [3, "Why would a test with only four cups, two of each, be unconvincing?", ["The tea would go cold before the end","Small tests always favour the poorest taster","A guesser gets a perfect score one time in six","Fisher's method only works with eight cups"], 2, "When luck alone succeeds this often, even a perfect score proves nothing, so test size matters."],
    [4, "What does Fisher's smoking argument show?", ["Randomised trials are generally unreliable","Smoking was never linked to lung cancer","Statisticians can't ask about hidden causes","Good methods don't cure wishful thinking"], 3, "Even the method's inventor let what he wanted to be true colour his judgement."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"She said she could taste which went in first. How do you test that fairly?"});

